# core.autocrlf is true on this machine, so without a rule here every text file
# comes back from a checkout with CRLF. Nothing in this repo wants that.
#
# windows/sync.sh is run by WSL's bash, where the CR becomes part of the token
# and line 12 dies with "set: pipefail: invalid option name".
#
# The files in sync.sh's PAIRS are compared byte-for-byte (`diff -q`) against
# their Windows-side copies, and every app that owns one of those copies — VS
# Code, PowerShell, oh-my-posh, GlazeWM — writes LF; both sides measure zero CR
# bytes. A CRLF working tree would therefore make every pair report DIFFERS on
# every run, burying the one signal that flags real drift.
#
# So: LF everywhere. `text=auto` still lets git detect binaries and leave them
# alone.
* text=auto eol=lf
